course: everyday
lesson: 310
title: Pavements, crossings, cars, trains, airports and bikes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On the street, think about what other people cannot see or do not expect. A toddler can dart into the road without warning, a driver may not stop at a crosswalk, and a truck driver cannot see a cyclist right beside the cab.
explain: So: hold a small child's hand and keep him on the side away from the traffic; at a crosswalk in the USA wait for cars to stop and make eye contact with the drivers before you cross; never ride up the inside of a truck at a junction.
explain: Safety gear only works when worn properly. A seat belt's lap part goes low across the hips and the shoulder part across the chest and shoulder, not the neck; a bike helmet sits snug and level, just above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled.
explain: Example: before a train trip in New York, a family agrees: «If we get separated, get off at the next station and wait on the platform.» A plan made beforehand stops panic.
fact: When walking a small child beside a busy road, hold their hand and keep them on the side away from the traffic.
fact: At a painted crosswalk in the USA, drivers must yield, but some do not, so wait for cars to stop and make eye contact before crossing.
fact: Lines on the road show drivers where to stop and where they may not park; double yellow lines in the UK mean no parking at any time.
fact: To ease car sickness, look out of the front window at the horizon, get fresh air and tell the adult early; reading or screens make it worse.
fact: A seat belt's lap part goes low across the hips and the shoulder part across the chest and shoulder, not the neck, because in a crash the belt must press on the strong bones of the hips and chest, not the soft neck or tummy.
fact: If the shoulder belt rubs your neck, you are probably too small for it; ask for a booster seat, a raised seat that lifts a child so the belt fits.
fact: Have your fare, travel card or contactless card ready before getting on the bus to keep the line moving.
fact: If separated from a parent on a train, follow the family plan: get off at the next station and wait, or stay on and tell staff.
fact: At airport security, put liquids in a small clear bag and follow the officers' instructions.
fact: Keep your passport safe with an adult, because you cannot travel abroad without it.
fact: A bike helmet must fit snugly, sit level just above the eyebrows and have the strap buckled; a loose helmet does not protect.
fact: Never ride up the inside of a truck or lorry at a junction: the driver cannot see you and it may turn.
fact: If your bike gets a puncture, walk it home or call a parent; you can learn to fix it later.
exercise: You are walking along a busy road with your little brother. What should you do? | Hold his hand and keep him on the side away from the traffic | Let him run on ahead so he does not get bored, and shout if a car comes | Let him walk next to the kerb so you can see him | Walk behind him and call if he goes near the road
exercise: Why should a toddler be kept away from the traffic side? | Small children can dart into the road without warning | Toddlers walk faster than older children and pull you towards the road | Cars are not allowed near small children | Drivers always stop for small children
exercise: A girl in Chicago is at a painted crosswalk. What should she do before crossing? | Wait for cars to stop and make eye contact with the drivers | Step out quickly, because cars must stop for her | Cross while looking at her phone, as drivers will see her | Run across before the cars reach the crosswalk
exercise: What do lines like stop lines and double yellow lines tell drivers? | Where to stop and where they may not park | Where children are allowed to play | How fast the cars are allowed to go on that road | Which way the wind is blowing
exercise: You start to feel carsick on a long drive. What helps most? | Look out of the front window at the horizon, get fresh air and tell the adult | Read your book more closely, so that your mind is taken off the bumpy road ahead | Play a game on a tablet until it passes | Say nothing until you are actually sick
exercise: Where should the shoulder part of a seat belt go? | Across the chest and shoulder | Across the neck | Behind the back | Under the arm
exercise: Where should the lap part of a seat belt go? | Low across the hips | High across the tummy | Around the knees | Across the chest
exercise: What should you do before you get on a bus? | Have your fare or travel card ready | Look for the money once you reach the driver | Ask the driver to wait while you find your purse | Get on and decide later whether to pay
exercise: A family is about to take a train in London. What is a smart thing to do beforehand? | Agree a plan for what to do if anyone gets separated | Agree that everyone will just look around the station if they get lost | Decide that the youngest will lead the way | Make no plan, because nobody ever gets lost
exercise: At airport security, what do you do with small bottles of liquid? | Put them in a small clear bag and follow the officers' instructions | Hide them right at the bottom of your bag, under your clothes and shoes | Drink them all as fast as you can | Hand them to a stranger in the queue
exercise: Who should keep your passport when you travel abroad as a child? | An adult travelling with you, keeping it safe | Anyone in the queue who offers to hold it | The person sitting next to you on the plane | Nobody, a child does not need a passport
exercise: Your bike helmet slides about on your head. What should you do? | Adjust it so it is snug, level, above the eyebrows, with the strap buckled | Push it right back so it sits on the back of your head and you can see better | Ride without it, since it does not fit | Leave the strap open so it is comfortable
exercise: A cyclist reaches a junction where a big truck is waiting. Where should she never ride? | Up the inside of the truck next to its cab | Behind the truck, a long way back from it | On the pavement, walking her bike | Waiting in line behind other traffic
exercise: Your bike gets a puncture far from home. What do you do? | Walk it home or call a parent | Keep riding on the flat tyre | Leave it on the street and walk away | Ask a stranger for a lift in their car
test: A boy in Glasgow is minding his four-year-old cousin on a pavement beside a busy road. Where should the cousin walk? | On the inside, holding his hand, away from the traffic | On the outside, next to the kerb, so he can keep an eye on him | A few steps ahead on their own | Behind him, holding his jacket
test: Which is true about crosswalks in the USA? | Drivers should stop, but some do not, so you check before crossing | Drivers always stop at every crosswalk, so you never need to look first | Crosswalks are only for cars, not people | You should cross diagonally across the junction
test: A girl reads comics in the back seat and starts to feel ill. What is the best advice? | Put the comic down, look at the horizon ahead and tell the driver | Read faster so the trip feels shorter and finish the comic before it gets worse | Close the windows to keep the car warm | Switch from the comic to a tablet game
test: In a crash, why does a seat belt across the neck cause harm? | The belt must hold the strong bones of the hips and chest, not the soft neck | A belt higher up on the neck protects better, because it holds your head still | Seat belts never matter in small crashes | It only matters for adults, not for children
test: A brother and sister are on a train in Sydney, but their mum did not get on in time. What should they do? | Follow the family plan, such as getting off at the next station and waiting, or telling staff | Pull the emergency handle to stop the train at once | Get off at a station far away and walk all the way back along the railway line to look for her | Go with a stranger who offers to help them find her
test: What could happen to a cyclist waiting beside the cab of a lorry that turns left in the UK? | The driver may not see her and could turn into her | The lorry must wait until she moves away | She is safe, because lorries never turn at junctions | The lorry driver always sees bikes in the mirror
test: A boy's helmet is pushed back so his forehead is showing. What is wrong? | It must sit level just above the eyebrows to protect his forehead | Nothing, a helmet protects you whatever position it is sitting in | It should cover his eyes for more protection | It should be worn without the strap
test: Why do people have their bus fare ready before getting on? | It keeps the line moving | Drivers will not let you on otherwise for an hour | Buses only stop for people holding money | It makes the bus go faster than the cars
test: Which is true about passports? | You cannot travel abroad without one, so an adult keeps it safe | You only need it to travel inside your own country, never to go abroad | Any card with your name will do instead | You can leave it at home if you know your name
test: Why do double yellow lines run along some UK roads? | They show drivers they may not park there | They show where children may cross | They mark the middle of a cycle lane | They show where buses must stop
test: walking my lil bro by the busy road | Hold his hand, keep him away from the traffic side | Let him walk along the kerb edge, it's like a balance game | Let him run ahead to the corner | Walk behind him so you can see him
test: carsick in the back, reading my book | Look out the front at the horizon and tell the adult | Read faster to finish quickly | Switch to a game on the tablet | Close the window so it is warmer, and read a bit more
test: helmet's a bit loose, it's fine | No, it must fit snug and be buckled | Yes, loose is more comfortable and safe | Yes, the strap is just for show | Fine if you ride slowly
test: lorry stopped at the lights, ill squeeze up the inside | No, the driver can't see you and it may turn | Yes, it's stopped at the lights so it's safe to go past | Yes, if you're quick | Only if you ring your bell
explain: A girl in California stepped onto a crosswalk because the cars «had to stop»; one driver did not, and she was knocked down and broke her leg.
explain: A boy in England wore his seat belt under his arm because it rubbed; in a small crash it pressed into his tummy and hurt him inside.
fact: Many children are hit on crossings by drivers who did not stop, which is why you wait until cars really have stopped.
fact: A seat belt worn in the wrong place can hurt the body in a crash.
test: green man's on but that car is still coming | Wait until the car stops | Go, you have the right | Run across quickly | Wave and walk
test: seatbelt rubs my neck so i put it under my arm | Keep it across your chest; ask for a booster seat | Under the arm is fine, it still holds you in a crash | Take it off on short trips | Put it behind your back
test: little sis wants to let go of my hand by the road | Keep holding her hand on the side away from cars | Let her walk ahead | Let her run to the corner | Hold her coat instead
test: did u hear a kid in LA stepped onto the crosswalk cos "cars have to stop" and "a car didn't". lesson? | Wait for cars to stop and make eye contact before crossing | Cars always stop at crosswalks | Run across really, really fast so that the cars can't hit you | Crosswalks make you completely safe
test: my aunt says reading in the car makes you "throw up for a whole week". what's prob exaggerated | The whole week; reading can make car sickness worse | That cars can make you sick | That fresh air helps | That reading can worsen it
test: kids say the boy who got a puncture and cried is "a baby". pass it on? | No, it's unkind; just walk the bike home or call a parent | Yes, he's a baby | Yes, and let his tyres down again | Yes, and post it in the class chat so that everyone knows
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct position of a seat belt? | The lap part low across the hips, the shoulder part across the chest | The lap part across the stomach, the shoulder part across the neck, both drawn tight | The belt behind the back entirely | The belt beneath both arms
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a small child is walked on the side away from traffic. | To keep the child further from the road | Because the child walks more slowly | Because the pavement is wider there | To allow the child to see the shops
exam: It is given that a passenger suffers car sickness. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Looking at the horizon, getting fresh air and telling the adult early | Reading a book attentively, in order to take one's mind off the motion | Watching a screen until it passes | Lying across the back seat unbelted
exam: State the meaning in the United Kingdom of double yellow lines along the road. | No parking at any time | Parking for buses only | Parking permitted at weekends | A lane reserved for bicycles
